index 92b175a..b1f021b 100644
--- a/koreader-plugin/README.md
+++ b/koreader-plugin/README.md
@@ -82,6 +82,14 @@ Position-only bookmarks (dogears) sync the same way:
 
 New bookmarks retain their source file. Mapping into a structurally different sibling requires equivalent chapter text; otherwise the bookmark remains unresolved. Legacy bookmarks without source-file provenance remain listed, but the selected-file web reader and delivery to another device withhold their position until a source-device sync or a new bookmark request establishes it. Device-side renames update BookOrbit; an already-linked second device keeps its existing title because bookmark exchange has no edit operation.
 
+## Exact tag lookup for local UI integrations
+
+`GET /api/v1/koreader/plugin/catalog/tag-matches?ids=1,2&tag=...` returns `{ "bookIds": [1] }`. It uses the normal KOReader credential headers, not anonymous access. Both parameters are mandatory: 1 to 200 positive safe-integer book IDs and a literal tag name of 1 to 200 characters. Invalid ID members and unknown query fields are rejected, not silently dropped. Tag matching is case-sensitive equality, not substring or genre/collection matching.
+
+Results are intersected with the supplied IDs, accessible libraries, present books and the authenticated user's content filters. The response selects IDs only and uses `Cache-Control: no-store`; it does not fetch details, covers or reading progress, or change any state. The caller must obtain IDs from its current-account local match maps and recheck file existence: the server cannot attest which files are on a device. Batch larger device lists rather than enumerating the remote library or fetching every book detail.
+
+Existing plugin client `query`/`request` and cancelable `runInSubprocess` methods can use this additive route. It requires a server containing this endpoint; older servers return 404. No deployment, plugin publication or database migration is part of the local implementation.
+
 ## Limitations
 
 - Web highlight changes reach a closed book only via a manual sweep or its next open.
